focus id

# @prism — the floor of the five-operation algebra.
#
# Root namespace. The five operations canonically named:
# focus, project, split, shift, settle. Per the substrate-pull
# rename (2026-06-04, Reed + Alex): zoom → shift; refract → settle.
# See docs/specs/prism-floor-and-the-grammar-rename.md.
#
# Per [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]: no `<= prism`
# redundancy. The recursive `prism @(id) { ... }` base case below
# is the canonical declaration form; `prism @prism { ... }`
# applies that form to itself (the self-declaration that closes
# the recursion).
#
# === The Connes spectral triple framing — the why ===
#
# Per [[prism-core-as-spectral-triple]] / [[spectral-triple-binary]]
# and docs/specs/prism-floor-and-the-grammar-rename.md §the 2026-06-04
# reframe: the five operations ARE the operational form of Connes'
# (A, H, D) spectral triple.
#
#   A = the five operations (the algebra acting on H).
#   H = the [[void-document]] (the substrate's Hilbert space:
#       graph quantum information manifold; λ₀ = 0 ground state;
#       eight dualities per Splinter/Narcissus).
#   D = the kintsugi flow (the Dirac operator; eⁿ⁺¹ ≤ eⁿ
#       monotone descent; c-theorem on graph Laplacians).
#
# Per-operation linear-algebraic meaning (canonical):
#
#   focus    = λ₀ eigenvalue identification (the ground state;
#              the spectrum's bottom — what the prism resolves to).
#   project  = orthogonal projection (idempotent restriction onto
#              a subspace; what the prism selects).
#   split    = orthogonal decomposition (the spectrum's partition;
#              what the prism separates into eigenspaces).
#   shift    = basis transformation (the functor; change of
#              coordinates on H; same bytes, different declared
#              shape). Per [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
#              the substrate-pull rename closes the loop with
#              `boot/std/{option,result}.mirror`'s `lift`.
#   settle   = monad-close / measurement collapse (the spectrum
#              read out; the verified-construction that the prism
#              produces). What the kintsugi flow descends to.
#
# The algebra is closed at five. Nothing the substrate adds is a
# sixth operation; everything composes from these.

prism @(id) {
  focus prism
  project prism
  split prism
  shift prism
  settle prism
}

prism @prism {
  focus focus
  project project
  split split
  shift shift
  settle settle
}

io tick(type) -> tock(type) { \ }

project in(prism)
project out(prism)

# === Parametric type forms — the five operations at the type layer ===
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-parametric-types-and-fp-heritage.md:
# the same five verbs that act on values also act on types, with
# the same names one universe up. Two are declared here as
# first-class parametric forms; the other three (focus(T),
# project(T), split(T)) fall out of the existing kernel forms
# (focus is type(id); project is field selection; split is the |
# disjunction in `type x = a | b`).
#
# shift(T): annotation-only parametric. Same bytes, different
# declared shape. Functor-shaped (basis transformation on H):
# shift satisfies identity (shift id = id) and composition
# (shift (f . g) = shift f . shift g). The runtime cost is zero
# by construction — the type checker distinguishes T(U1) from
# T(U2) via content address; the bytes don't change. This is the
# change-of-coordinates operation of A acting on H.
#
# settle(T): verified-construction parametric. Building a
# settle(T) value IS the proof that the construction settled
# (monad-close / measurement collapse). Monad-shaped: settle
# satisfies left identity, right identity, and associativity.
# Content-addressing gives associativity for free at composition
# time (oid concatenation is associative; the substrate enforces
# it). This is the readout under D's monotone descent.
#
# Both are type-layer applications of the existing five Prism
# operations. No new primitives — the algebra is closed at five.
# The @epistemologic/property/laws/{functor,monad} grammars
# verify the laws structurally.
shift shift(type) -> type { \ }
settle settle(type) -> type { \ }

out in
out id
out @
out @prism
out shift
out settle
